  "response": "Adjudication between socialization accounts and gatekeeping accounts of persistent gender disparities in STEM begins with the recognition that even the descriptive baseline is disputed. Either women earn a majority of United States bachelor's degrees awarded in the biological sciences, or they earn a minority of them: one of these two descriptions must be correct, and they cannot both be correct. The two explanatory traditions differ chiefly in where they locate the decisive mechanism along the educational and professional pipeline. On the current evidence, at least one of the following holds, and perhaps both hold together: parents give girls less encouragement toward mathematics than they give boys, and hiring and promotion committees in academic science apply evaluation criteria that disadvantage women candidates.\n\nDeciding between these loci requires research designs that hold candidate quality constant while varying only the applicant's apparent sex. Goldin and Rouse found that blind auditions raised women's chances of advancing in symphony orchestra hiring rounds, work that came earlier than the faculty-evaluation experiments considered below. Researchers use correspondence experiments with randomly assigned applicant names to separate discrimination from applicant differences, and the adoption of that technique led Moss-Racusin and colleagues to publish a 2012 randomized experiment on science faculty evaluations in the Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences. The findings of that publication confirm that male and female faculty in the 2012 lab-manager experiment displayed comparable levels of bias against the female applicant.\n\nBy contrast with that uniformity of bias across evaluator sex, the strategy of randomly assigning applicant names resulted in a further body of work, most prominently Williams and Ceci's report of a national experiment in which faculty preferred hypothetical women candidates for tenure-track positions at a two-to-one ratio. The explanatory field is also constrained from a different direction. Differences in average mathematical ability account for the underrepresentation of women among academic scientists. A National Academies consensus report determined that harassment and hostile climate, rather than ability differences, drive substantial attrition of women from academic science, and that determination cannot be squared with the account resting on average ability differences; the two explanations cannot both stand.\n\nThe consensus report's determination is itself the evidential basis for a narrower claim about the mechanism of departure. Sexual harassment is a documented driver of women's attrition from academic science careers, and that documented loss of trained scientists led the National Science Foundation to fund institutional-change grants aimed at women's advancement in academic science through its ADVANCE program. Chronology matters here as well: Title IX of the United States Education Amendments was enacted in 1992, which is to say before the funding program just described began awarding its grants. The upshot for scholarly consensus is that the socialization and gatekeeping accounts are not adjudicated by appeal to aggregate participation figures, which admit of competing descriptions, but by experimental and institutional evidence bearing directly on evaluation and climate. Randomized manipulations of applicant identity isolate the behavior of gatekeepers from any differences in the candidates themselves, and consensus reviews of attrition locate the mechanism in the conditions women encounter after entry rather than in the dispositions they bring to it.",
